  "summary": "The ACLU Foundation of San Diego & Imperial Counties and the Partnership for the Advancement of New Americans filed suit seeking an injunction to force the city of San Diego to stop using ALPRs, terminate related contracts, uninstall streetlight cameras operating as readers, and delete stored data. Plaintiffs argue SDPD's dense camera network and month-long retention violate the California constitutional right to privacy, citing a wrongful identification and the May mosque shooting as examples of harm and ineffectiveness.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "They're asking the court to make the city stop using ALPRs and terminate contracts associated with the technology, while uninstalling all streetlight cameras that operate as readers and deleting all the stored data that has been collected by the systems.",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "By retaining ALPR data for a month-long period, the plaintiffs claim SDPD can create a pattern based on people's movements throughout the city",
      "paragraph_idx": 7
    },
    {
      "quote": "to their knowledge this is the only local lawsuit targeting a city in an attempt to discontinue its use of ALPRs under the California constitution",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    }
  ],
